<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#prakrit">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prakrit</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Prakrits</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prakrit is the name for the vernacular Middle Indo-Aryan languages of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> but simpler in sound and grammar. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s edicts and many early royal inscriptions are in Prakrit, and Pali and the languages of Jain scripture are forms of it. Courts used it in inscriptions and coins until Sanskrit largely replaced it from the 4th century CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prakrit.jpg" alt="Prakrit" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 6th century CE engraver. inked impression and photograph by J.F. Fleet (1847 – 21 February 1917) in 1888 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Word_for_Prakrit_Praakritee_in_the_Mandsaur_stone_inscription_of_Yashodharman-Vishnuvardhana_532_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
